+The default `cargo xtask web` remains a standard, single-threaded artifact. An optional
+`cargo xtask web --threads` additionally packages a shared-memory artifact in `dist/mt/`.
+This is an application build for **ordinary Chrome and other compatible browsers**, not a custom
+browser. End users need no Rust toolchain, browser flags or extensions. The server must provide
+cross-origin isolation (COOP `same-origin`, COEP `require-corp`) and a secure context (HTTPS or
+localhost). External resources must permit the applicable CORS/CORP requests.
+
+Developers building the optional artifact need a separate pinned toolchain:
+
+```sh
+rustup toolchain install nightly-2026-10-28 --profile minimal --component rust-src --target wasm32-unknown-unknown
+cargo xtask web --threads --serve 8765
+```
+
+The normal artifact still uses the developer's standard compiler. Only the optional artifact
+uses nightly `build-std`, atomics and `wasm-bindgen-rayon` 1.3.0. Startup initializes up to eight
+Rayon workers, with shared WASM memory capped at 2 GiB. Existing parallel inner loops can use
+those workers; the frame scheduler remains cooperative and synchronous joins can still stall
+the UI. This is not a dedicated rendering-worker architecture or a promise of smooth playback.
+
+When isolation/shared memory is unavailable, startup selects the standard artifact. A threaded
+startup failure reloads once with `?nothreads` before editing begins, releasing partial workers.
+`?nothreads` explicitly selects the standard artifact; `?threads=1` through `?threads=8` bound the
+optional worker count for controlled comparisons. `filmcraft.info()` reports `crossOriginIsolated`,
+`threadedBuild`, actual `rayonThreads` and the distinct cooperative `frameWorkers` model.
 Commands that need a thread on the desktop (render previews, proxies, Project Manager, mask
 tracking) report an error on the web for now.
 
